From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] power: supply: max77705: port Samsung charger setup and BC1.2

The mainline MAX77705 charger driver leaves the chip in a state that
differs from what Samsung's firmware programs on the Galaxy Z Flip3, and
it sets no current limit at all when a cable is plugged in. Port the parts
of Samsung's driver that change what the user sees.

Initialisation, all values and register bits taken from
drivers/battery/charger/max77705_charger/max77705_charger.c in Samsung's
kernel (max77705_charger_initialize(), lines 903-1043):

  - switching frequency comes from DT, 3 MHz on this board (stock
    charger,fsw = <0>), instead of a hardcoded 1.5 MHz
  - the top-off timer comes from DT, 70 min as stock sets it (stock
    battery,topoff_time = <70>), instead of a hardcoded 30 min
  - BAT to SYS OCP comes from DT and can be disabled (stock
    battery,chg_ocp_current = <0>), with its 100 ms blanking bit
  - RECYCLE_EN is set (CNFG_01 bit 3) and EN_AICL is left enabled
    (CNFG_06 bit 4), as stock does for non-factory builds
  - UNOILIM is set to 1.0 A (CNFG_05 bits 6:4)
  - VCHGIN is 4.6 V while a charger is present and 4.5 V otherwise, and
    the CHGIN input is selected (CNFG_12), which is what stock's ONLINE
    handler does at lines 1626-1634
  - FMBST is enabled and FGSRC is 0 (CNFG_07), ship mode and auto ship
    mode are off, so that the bootloader's auto ship mode bit (CNFG_03
    bit 6) cannot switch the rail off

Cable detection: the USB-C block at I2C address 0x25 runs BC1.2 on its own
and reports the port type in REG_BC_STATUS (0x08). The MFD driver already
owns that block as max77705->regmap_muic; the charger now refers to it
through maxim,pmic and asks for the PMIC's USBC interrupt source (the
second interrupt) so that CHGTypI and VBUSDetI drive it instead of polling.
The per-port currents are DT arrays, in the order the hardware reports the
port type: SDP, CDP, DCP. They default to no change, so boards that do not
describe them keep their previous behaviour.

Also fix the CHGIN_LIM encoding: the register holds the limit in 25 mA
steps minus one step (stock writes current / 25 - 1, 500 mA is 0x13);
mainline wrote current / 25, one step high. Both the property setter and
the cable code now go through one helper. The charge current limit is
clamped to 3.15 A, the highest value its 6-bit field can hold.

The watchdog stays disabled: stock only kicks it from its 10 s and 30 s
polling loop, and enabling it without a kick path would silently stop
charging once the AP sleeps past the timeout.

Signed-off-by: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
---
